page();h('Abstract of Praxis',1);p('Identifying Autonomous AI APT',center=True)
p('Autonomous artificial intelligence agents can perform cybersecurity actions, but distinguishing their behavior from human-operated activity remains sensitive to environment and collection method. This Praxis proposes a matched laboratory study of defender-visible host and network features during APT-like activity. Human red-team participants and autonomous agents will pursue equivalent objectives over five collection days using a published AWS range design. The intended external AI controller is an NVIDIA DGX Spark running locally supplied Gemma and Qwen variants. Command diversity and command-sequence transition entropy are the principal hypothesized features. Logistic regression will test transfer to unseen groups; support vector machines, trees, random forests, XGBoost and a tabular transformer will be compared under common grouped evaluation. EDA will assess completeness, dependence, feature stability and collection shortcuts. Trial-level macro-F1 is primary; false AI labels on humans, class recall, balanced accuracy, calibration and cost provide supporting evidence.')
p('ILLUSTRATIVE OUTCOME ONLY: A capacity example assumes 120 primary trials and 24 controls, including 40 locked primary trials. An invented SVM confusion matrix yields macro-F1 of 0.825 and a human false-AI rate of 0.150. These values demonstrate reporting; they are not findings or forecasts with validated uncertainty. Formal hypothesis decisions remain pending.',True)
p('The intended contribution is a reproducible attribution protocol with explicit provenance and transfer limits. Positive, negative and inconclusive outcomes are possible; operator attribution is distinct from intrusion detection and geopolitical attribution.')
