chap(1,'Introduction')
h('1.1 Background')
p('Defenders observe intrusion effects without necessarily knowing whether a person or autonomous agent selected each action. Process creation, authentication and network connections offer possible behavioral evidence. However, humans, scripts and AI agents can use the same tools. Attribution requires controlled comparisons rather than treating rapid activity or one executable as proof of AI control. Alt (2026) examined automation classes in SSH interactions, while Ediga and Chattopadhyay (2026) examined AI-family terminal fingerprints. These motivate, but do not establish, the present target-side generalization claim.')
h('1.2 Research Motivation')
p('A useful classifier must retain informative patterns when the operator, configuration or collection setting changes. A model that recognizes an archive can appear accurate while failing on another source. The project’s real-record feasibility screen exhibited this problem: within-source performance dropped during transfer, and the two AI sources were highly distinguishable. The practical objective is therefore a bounded assessment of useful features and methods, not an artificially favorable model ranking. Negative findings can identify measurement failures or limits of attribution.')
h('1.3 Problem Statement')
p('It remains uncertain which defender-visible host and network behaviors reliably distinguish human-operated from autonomous-AI APT-like activity in a matched multi-host laboratory, and whether classifiers transfer to unseen operators and AI configurations rather than exploiting collection artifacts.',italic=True)
h('1.4 Thesis Statement')
p('This study proposes that autonomous AI attacks produce measurable behavioral patterns that distinguish them from human-operated attacks, even when the human operator or AI configuration has not previously been observed. The study will test this proposition using target-side telemetry collected during matched, controlled APT-like laboratory trials.',italic=True)
p('Alt (2026) and Ediga and Chattopadhyay (2026) provide supporting motivation through automation-class classification and AI-family fingerprinting. Neither demonstrates the full proposed matched autonomous multi-host comparison. The thesis is a proposition for testing.')
h('1.5 Research Objectives')
p('The objectives are to define reproducible target-side features, assess their incremental value, evaluate transfer after timing/tool controls, and compare classifiers under common information and tuning limits. Supporting outputs include a provenance-aware data schema, extraction specification and reproducibility record. Public outputs must not expose credentials or identifiable participant information.')
h('1.6 Research Questions and Hypotheses')
rqs=['Which target-side host and network behavioral feature families distinguish human-operated from autonomous-AI APT-like trials under matched conditions?','Which classifiers retain useful behavioral attributes for classification performance on autonomous-AI APT data?','Among XGBoost, a decision tree, a random forest, SVM and a tabular transformer, which provides the strongest trial-level human-versus-autonomous-AI classification?']
hyps=['Command diversity and command-sequence transition entropy improve classification of human-operated versus autonomous-AI APT-like activity compared with a predefined baseline.','Logistic regression retains useful behavioral attributes and achieves above-baseline classification performance on previously unseen autonomous-AI configurations and human operators.','SVM achieves higher trial-level macro-F1 than XGBoost, a decision tree, a random forest and a tabular transformer for human-versus-autonomous-AI classification.']
for i in range(3):p(f'RQ{i+1}: '+rqs[i]);p(f'H{i+1}: '+hyps[i])
p('These hypotheses were approved October 24, 2026 after exploratory analysis. They are not retrospective preregistrations; formal data must supply a separate evaluation. Lack of support does not prove equality, and the largest point estimate alone does not support H3.')
h('1.7 Scope of Research')
p('The population is authorized attack trials on a controlled range. APT-like denotes staged objectives and persistence within the exercise, not attribution to a real threat organization. Benign and scripted controls remain separate. A binary model trained on attack trials is not a general intrusion detector. External controller placement describes the network boundary, not unrestricted Internet targeting.')
p('The author supplied huihui_ai/qwen2.5-coder-abliterate:32b, local identifier 36711483afce, displayed size 19 GB; and gemma4-ablit:latest, local identifier 63f914bef7f4, displayed size 16 GB. These entries identify intended artifacts but do not verify full digests, base lineage, modifications, capabilities or completed runs.')
h('1.8 Research Limitations')
p('Five days constrain independent-group counts. Multiple windows from one participant or configuration do not create independent subjects. Two checkpoints provide limited family diversity. Prompt holdouts within one checkpoint are configuration transfer, not unseen-family transfer. Boundary latency, task completion and sensor loss may change apparent separability; these limitations must remain visible.')
h('1.9 Organization of Praxis')
p('Chapter 2 reviews prior work and bounds novelty. Chapter 3 specifies the GMR, collection, EDA, algorithms, equations and robustness. Chapter 4 distinguishes actual exploratory findings from illustrative formal-result tables. Chapter 5 develops conditional discussion and conclusions. Appendices define evidence replacement and reproduction requirements.')
chap(2,'Literature Review')
h('2.1 Review Method and Evidence Status')
p('A focused update on October 24, 2026 examined institutional repositories, arXiv, publisher records, official software documentation and GW guidance. Search concepts included human versus autonomous AI attacker classification, terminal behavior fingerprinting, AI honeypot evaluation, matched red-team data and AWS attack ranges. Citation chaining used the existing project review. This is not an exhaustive systematic review with a registered search protocol. Preprints, theses, accepted work and published papers are distinguished, and source metadata inspection is not represented as full-text verification.')
p('Sources were retained when relevant to operator behavior, comparative evaluation, data provenance or the selected algorithms. Unverified news and unrelated prompt-jailbreak benchmarks were not treated as evidence for attribution. The final literature audit must revisit full texts and new publications before claiming priority. No single citation proves novelty.')
h('2.2 Automation Classes and AI Fingerprints')
p('Alt (2026) is the closest direct comparator because it classifies human, conventional automation and AI-assisted activity using SSH honeypot behavior. It prevents a claim that this study is the first to distinguish human and AI-associated sessions. The proposed extension concerns autonomous execution, matched multi-host tasks, defender-side measurement and operator/configuration holdouts. AI-assisted and autonomous labels require different intervention evidence.')
p('Trace attributes AI model families from terminal sequences and reports weaker performance on unseen scaffolds (Ediga & Chattopadhyay, 2026). It makes model-specific fingerprints an explicit concern: a human-versus-AI classifier may learn one implementation rather than a transferable property. Its attribution target differs from the binary operator-type target, but its transfer results motivate scaffold/configuration controls.')
h('2.3 Recent Behavioral and Deception Research')
p('Honey for the Agent examines nine open-weight models across prompts and SSH environments. Its institutional accepted-paper record describes distinctive command-selection fingerprints and effects of environmental cues (Maddaloni et al., in press). It supports feature hypotheses and source-transfer checks but lacks the matched human cohort proposed here. The citation retains its in-press status rather than inventing final pagination.')
p('Honeyquest for LLMs compares models with a human baseline on an identical reconnaissance-query instrument (Prinos et al., 2026). Shared-stimulus human–AI comparisons therefore already exist. Judgment about deception differs from continuous defender-side measurements of live multi-stage activity. The study helps delimit novelty and motivates control of task framing and attacker knowledge.')
p('The September 2026 Rouxii preprint evaluates deception-aware AI pentesters, showing that knowledge and prompting affect their interactions (Cordeiro et al., 2026). This strengthens the requirement to pin prompts and skills. It also cautions against generalizing a classifier from one agent-policy setting to all autonomous attackers. Rouxii does not itself establish the proposed human-versus-AI defender classifier.')
h('2.4 Data and Experimental Infrastructure')
p('GAMBiT provides human red-team cyber-range observations with participant-linked and multimodal records (Beltz et al., 2025). It supports behavior analysis and appropriate grouping of repeated observations. A corresponding 2026 Data in Brief record was located during the update; this draft uses the verified author-complete preprint citation for the experimental description. Different tasks, interventions and environments prevent treating its human traces and unrelated AI archives as a matched study.')
p('Cochise offers a reference planner–executor harness and released trajectories for autonomous penetration-testing research (Happe & Cito, 2026). It is useful as experimental infrastructure and a separate source for feasibility checks. Its controller traces are not native target-side telemetry. The citation does not establish deployment of the intended custom model or AWS topology.')
p('Splunk Attack Range supports reproducible instrumented cloud labs including AWS, with Terraform/Ansible provisioning and optional network visibility (Splunk, n.d.). It is a candidate infrastructure baseline, not evidence of this project’s deployment. Its exact commit, template, modifications and sensors must be recorded to make an implemented range reproducible.')
h('2.5 Models and Evaluation')
p('SVMs learn margin-based separation and can use kernels for nonlinear relationships (Cortes & Vapnik, 1995). Random forests combine randomized trees (Breiman, 2001); XGBoost learns regularized additive trees (Chen & Guestrin, 2016). Their different inductive assumptions should be compared using common information. Tabular benchmark evidence motivates strong tree baselines without guaranteeing a winner in this small grouped study (Grinsztajn et al., 2022). FT-Transformer supplies a concrete numerical-feature comparison, and its authors identify no universally superior family (Gorishniy et al., 2021).')
p('Group-aware validation and training-only preprocessing protect against dependence and leakage. Correlated features can obscure individual permutation importance, making retrained ablation and family-level analysis important companions (scikit-learn developers, n.d.-a, n.d.-b). The earlier project transformer was an FT-style engineering implementation; it remains distinct from a qualified replication of the published baseline.')
tab('Table 2.1 Closest literature and proposed extension',['Source and status','Established scope','Remaining scope here'],[['Alt 2026 thesis','SSH automation classes','Matched autonomous multi-host telemetry'],['Trace 2026 preprint','AI-family terminal fingerprints','Human versus AI and unseen operators'],['Honey in press','AI fingerprints across settings','Matched human cohort and sensors'],['Honeyquest 2026 preprint','Humans and LLMs on shared queries','Live multi-stage target observations'],['Rouxii 2026 preprint','Knowledge changes AI deception behavior','Attribution across prompt/skill changes'],['GAMBiT dataset preprint','Human range behavior','Equivalent autonomous cohort'],['Cochise harness paper','Agent infrastructure and trajectories','Same-range human comparison']],[1.5,2.1,2.4])
h('2.6 Novelty Synthesis')
p('The candidate contribution is the integration of matched human and autonomous-AI operations, multi-host defender-visible measurement, grouped transfer evaluation, feature ablations and automation controls. None of the inspected records establishes this exact combination; that bounded statement is not proof of universal priority. Alt is the closest problem comparator. Trace and Honey explain the fingerprint/transfer risk. Honeyquest prevents overstating the novelty of shared human–AI stimuli. The final contribution must be narrowed to what is actually implemented and supported.')
chap(3,'Methodology')
h('3.1 Design and Data Provenance')
p('The study is a proposed controlled matched-trial experiment. A trial is an authorized attempt from a restored range state with an objective, actor, observation budget and outcome record. Events and windows are nested within trials; trials are nested within participants or AI configurations. Human and AI arms receive equivalent access, objectives and allowed tools. Five days describe the collection schedule, not five independent statistical observations.')
p('The project refers to a synthetic dataset because the range and scenario are constructed. For scientific reporting, the manifest will distinguish measured laboratory telemetry, generated synthetic records and illustrative manuscript values. Telemetry produced by real people and agents in the range is real experimental data from a synthetic scenario. The earlier 880-row fixture is generated simulation. The red result tables are manuscript illustrations and do not constitute measured data.')
red('Capacity example: 12 human operators and 12 AI configurations, each completing five trials, produce 120 primary trials over five days. Add 12 scripted and 12 benign controls. These are assumed counts, not recruitment, executed trials or a power-justified sample. Confirm capacity after the planned 24-execution pilot before fixing final size.')
p('Configuration identity combines checkpoint, scaffold, prompt, skill bundle and inference settings. Configurations from the same checkpoint remain related at the family level. Preserve both IDs. Holding out a prompt or seed does not establish transfer to a new model family. Pilot precision and learning curves should inform whether more independent groups are needed; extra windows cannot substitute for them.')
h('3.2 AWS Range and External Agent')
red('Candidate range repository: https://github.com/splunk/attack_range. Its exact AWS template, commit, region, images, service inventory and deployment status remain unresolved. The diagram is a proposed architecture.')
p('Human execution hosts and the DGX Spark agent will use equivalent authorized external entry conditions. The management/reset path is separate from the exercise path. The range will capture host/process, authentication and network evidence with an independent truth manifest. Addresses and account identities may support joins in restricted evidence but are excluded or normalized in predictors. External placement must not make source addresses, NAT or latency direct class labels.')
p('NVIDIA (n.d.) documents DGX Spark as a local AI system. The supplied model listings do not verify throughput or memory headroom. Record actual hardware, runtime, quantization, context limits and concurrency before scheduling. The Gemma technical report supplies family background, not validation of the local gemma4-ablit variant (Gemma Team, 2026). The local suffixes do not establish any particular modification; the study records provenance rather than inferring capabilities from names.')
red('Provisional roles: gemma4-ablit:latest is primary; huihui_ai/qwen2.5-coder-abliterate:32b is a cross-model comparison. The skills repository, scaffold, full digests, prompts, temperatures, action limits and runtime settings must be supplied and frozen. Human tactical assistance creates an assisted/interrupted record rather than an autonomous one.')
